--- before/chapters/01-how-decisions-should-be-made-and-how-they-actually-are.qmd
+++ chapters/01-how-decisions-should-be-made-and-how-they-actually-are.qmd
@@ -197,7 +197,9 @@
 
 Once an outcome arrives, judging the decision seems easier: success appears to vindicate the choice and failure to condemn it. But a good decision can produce a bad outcome, and a poor decision can produce a favorable one. Rewarding luck or punishing a defensible risk can teach the wrong lesson. A useful review must separate process quality from result quality.
 
-An investor may buy after one enthusiastic post and make money. Another may diversify on defensible evidence and lose during an unexpected market shock. A surgeon can recommend the treatment best supported at the time and still encounter a rare complication. **Outcome bias** judges the earlier decision mainly by its result; **hindsight bias** makes the result seem more predictable after it occurs (Baron & Hershey, 1988; Fischhoff, 1975).
+Baron and Hershey (1988) tested whether people could keep that distinction in view. Participants evaluated hypothetical medical decisions and gambles whose risks were described before the outcome. A favorable result made the decision-maker's thinking seem better than an unfavorable result did, even when the information available at the time of choice was the same. Participants were allowing information the decision-maker could not have known to change their assessment of the earlier reasoning.
+
+This is **outcome bias**: judging a decision by how it turned out rather than by the information and reasoning available when it was made. **Hindsight bias** adds a related distortion, making the result seem more predictable after it occurs (Fischhoff, 1975). An unexpected market shock can turn a defensible investment into a loss; a lucky gain cannot establish that an impulsive purchase was well judged.
 
 Feedback therefore does not automatically create learning. A useful review reconstructs what was known, which alternatives were considered, what was predicted, what mattered, and what could have changed the decision. A dated record protects the earlier judgment from a narrator who already knows the ending. [*Narrator After Choice*](07-the-narrator-after-choice-why-reasons-are-not-always-causes.qmd#the-narrator-after-choice-why-reasons-are-not-always-causes) develops that problem; [*Decision Hygiene*](41-decision-hygiene-build-a-process-that-can-learn.qmd#decision-hygiene-build-a-process-that-can-learn) turns it into organizational practice.
 

