-The contrasting loops in @tbl-self-fulfilling-self-defeating make “Was the forecast right?” an incomplete review question. Ask also: **Through what actions did the forecast change exposure, effort, resources, or other people’s responses?** A label such as “promising” or “unreliable” may influence the conditions under which the person will next be judged. The review must distinguish that treatment from evidence about performance under other conditions.
-
-## Expectation in the body
-
-A treatment supplies more than its active ingredient: it also supplies information about what to expect. If that information changes pain or other symptoms, explaining the outcome requires tracing the context as well as the treatment. The same challenge appears in management and education, where expectations can change both a person’s response and the opportunities offered to them.
-
-![Expectations can affect outcomes through four parallel pathways: physiological response, the person's behavior, other people's responses, and the allocation of resources or opportunities. Feedback may reinforce the expectation in a self-fulfilling pattern or counteract the predicted outcome in a self-defeating pattern.](../figures/expectation-loop.svg){#fig-expectation-loop fig-alt="An expectation branches into four parallel pathways: physiological response, behavior and effort, other people's responses, and resources and opportunities. The pathways converge on changed outcomes and evidence. A feedback path distinguishes self-fulfilling reinforcement from self-defeating prevention."}
-
-In @fig-expectation-loop, one pathway may operate alone, or several may combine. Attention and interpretation can mediate each pathway—for example, symptom monitoring can alter physiology, and a manager's interpretation can alter resource allocation. The key identification question is which pathway in @tbl-08-expectation-pathways changed what followed (Merton, 1948; Harris & Rosenthal, 1985; Kirsch, 1985).
-
-| Pathway | What the expectation changes | Illustration |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Physiology | Stress, pain, fatigue, satiety, and arousal regulation. | A nocebo warning can amplify symptom experience. |
-| The person’s behavior | Practice, avoidance, disclosure, trust, persistence, and feedback seeking. | Low expectations can reduce effort and opportunities to learn. |
-| Other people’s responses | How teachers, managers, peers, or clinicians respond. | A teacher may offer more challenge, patience, or feedback to a pupil expected to improve. |
-| Resources and opportunities | Time, budget, access, roles, and information. | A forecast of failure may lead a project to be underfunded. |
-: Four routes from expectation to outcome; a change in one can affect another {#tbl-08-expectation-pathways}
-
-**Placebo effects** are changes attributable to treatment context, including expectations and learning, beyond the specific action of the treatment. **Nocebo effects** are adverse changes attributable to such contextual processes. They can accompany active treatment as well as an inert intervention (Benedetti, 2008; Colloca & Barsky, 2020; Colloca & Miller, 2011). Improvement in a placebo group alone does not establish a placebo effect: natural recovery, regression to the mean, and other influences may also contribute.
-
-Pain research provides one example of plausible pathways. Expectations can affect anxiety, attention to symptoms, and endogenous pain regulation, although the mechanisms and size of effects vary by outcome and procedure (Finniss et al., 2010; Kirsch, 1985).
-
-Risk communication should therefore preserve accurate probabilities and explain what support is available. A clinician can describe both the proportion likely to experience a side effect and the proportion unlikely to experience it, using the same outcome and time horizon.
-
-::: {.callout-note .research-lens icon=false collapse=true}
-## Research Lens: expectations can alter reported experience
-
-A tasting seems to offer direct evidence about a product’s qualities. Yet information about the product can enter the experience being judged, making description and experience harder to separate. Price and product information have changed reported pleasantness, preference, or performance in controlled tasting and consumer tasks (Lee et al., 2006; Plassmann et al., 2008; Shiv et al., 2005). Information supplied beforehand can help construct the experience rather than merely justify it afterward. Studies have also examined mindset effects on health and physiology (Crum & Langer, 2007; Crum et al., 2011).[^ch05-expectancy-mechanisms]
-:::
-
-Information supplied before an experience can influence how it is interpreted. Evaluating its effects requires measuring the relevant outcome: reported experience, physiological change, or lasting health benefit.
-
-[]{#subliminal-tapes-and-perceived-improvement}
-
-A self-help product provides a useful test of this distinction. Greenwald and colleagues (1991) examined audiotapes advertised as improving memory or self-esteem through subliminal messages. Across three replications, 237 participants completed both baseline and follow-up assessments. The researchers varied the tape's label separately from its actual content: a recording containing memory messages could carry a self-esteem label, and vice versa. Participants were asked to listen daily for a month, allowing the researchers to separate what the product contained from what users expected it to do.
-
-The memory tests and self-esteem scales did not show the benefits claimed for the corresponding subliminal content. Participants' judgments about whether they had improved nevertheless tended to follow the label: those given a memory label were more likely to report a memory benefit; those given a self-esteem label were more likely to report a self-esteem benefit. The visible promise shaped the perceived result.
-
-Scores generally increased between baseline and follow-up, but that change alone cannot establish a causal placebo effect. Repeated testing, participation in the study, or other changes could contribute. The experiment separates three questions: Did the advertised ingredient work? Did measured outcomes change? Did users believe they had improved? A favorable answer to the last question need not establish the first. The finding concerns these tapes and measured outcomes; it does not imply that expectation effects on pain or other symptoms are merely imagined.
-
-[]{#illness-after-a-deadline}
-
-Outside a treatment setting, the timing of symptoms can also invite an expectation-based explanation. Someone working through weeks of unavoidable responsibility may think, “I will get sick as soon as this is over.” During the demanding period, they feel able to keep going; once the work ends, fatigue, a headache, or cold symptoms become hard to ignore. The timing makes an expectation-based explanation tempting: did anticipating illness help bring it about?
-
-Reports of this pattern have been studied as **leisure sickness**. In an exploratory Dutch survey, about 3% of respondents recognized themselves in descriptions of sickness during weekends or vacations. A separate comparison of cases and controls found differences in perceived workload, responsibility, and difficulty relaxing (Vingerhoets et al., 2002).
-
-Several explanations could fit. Symptoms may have received less attention during absorbing work; changes in stress, sleep, or routine may matter; an infection acquired earlier may become symptomatic later. Expecting a familiar post-deadline collapse could also direct attention toward symptoms.
-
-
-## Ability beliefs and performance
-
-Difficulty should help a student identify what needs practice. But if confusion is taken as proof of inability, it may instead become a reason to avoid the task. Another student who sees confusion as part of learning may seek a new strategy. The same obstacle can therefore open or close a path to improvement. The question is which beliefs support useful action and what support makes that action possible.
-
-Self-efficacy, a concept developed by Bandura, refers to people’s beliefs about their capability to organize and execute actions required to achieve goals (Bandura, 1977, 1997). Self-efficacy affects effort, persistence, resilience, emotional response, and willingness to take on challenge. People with higher self-efficacy are more likely to approach difficult tasks, persist after setbacks, and recover from failure.
-
-Self-efficacy is not the same as empty confidence. It is most useful when grounded in mastery experiences, modeling, feedback, and realistic strategies. False confidence can be dangerous. But low confidence can also become self-defeating if it prevents the very effort that would build ability.
-
-Dweck’s work on implicit theories of intelligence makes a related point. A fixed mindset treats ability as relatively stable; a growth mindset treats ability as developable through effort, strategy, and support (Dweck & Leggett, 1988; Dweck, 2006). Students with growth-oriented beliefs may respond differently to difficulty because failure carries a different meaning. Blackwell, Trzesniewski, and Dweck (2007) found that growth mindset interventions could improve motivation and academic trajectories in some settings.
-
-Meta-analytic work finds modest, context-dependent mindset effects (Sisk et al., 2018). Yeager and colleagues (2019) found that a brief intervention raised end-of-ninth-grade GPAs in core subjects among lower-achieving US students by an estimated 0.10 points on a four-point scale relative to the control group. In the follow-up covering both lower- and higher-achieving students, advanced mathematics enrollment in tenth grade was 36% in the intervention group, compared with 33% in the control group. The grade benefit depended partly on school context. The practical lesson is to pair beliefs about development with real opportunities, strategies, support, and feedback.
-
-::: {.callout-note icon=false collapse=true}
-## Research note: learning that an action can help
-
-Early learned-helplessness research found that animals exposed to uncontrollable adverse events could later fail to escape even when escape was possible (Seligman, 1975). Human theories extended the account to explanations of adversity, including whether a cause is judged stable or widespread (Abramson et al., 1978).
-
-Maier and Seligman's (2016) later review substantially revised the original animal account: passivity under prolonged aversive stimulation need not itself be learned, while detecting control can recruit processes that protect active responding.
-:::
-
-Expectations also shape performance through other people. Rosenthal and Jacobson’s famous study, Pygmalion in the Classroom, suggested that teacher expectations could influence student achievement (Rosenthal & Jacobson, 1968). Teachers were told that certain randomly selected students were likely to show intellectual growth. Those students later showed greater IQ gains than classmates who had not been singled out, especially among younger children. Later reviews find moderate teacher expectancy effects that vary across settings (Jussim & Harber, 2005).
-
-Expectancy effects can operate through multiple channels: teachers may provide more warmth, more challenging material, more response opportunities, more detailed feedback, and more patience to students from whom they expect growth (Harris & Rosenthal, 1985). Students then respond to the richer environment.
-
-The same logic applies to management. Eden (1992) described Pygmalion effects in organizations: leaders’ high expectations can improve subordinate performance when they produce confidence, support, challenge, and opportunity. McNatt’s (2000) meta-analysis found evidence for Pygmalion effects in management contexts. Low expectations can produce the opposite, sometimes called a Golem effect, where negative expectations undermine performance.
-
-“High expectations” need credible belief in capacity combined with resources, coaching, autonomy, and feedback. Empty slogans can become blame: “If you failed, you must not have believed enough.” That is not decision science; it is moralized optimism.
-
-For the manager, that means pairing a demanding assignment with clear authority, resources, and a way to obtain feedback.
-
-::: {.callout-note .research-lens icon=false collapse=true}
-## Research Lens: identity changes what difficulty means
-
-A hard task can mean “I am learning,” “I do not belong,” or “I may confirm what others expect of people like me.” To interpret performance, we need to know which meaning the task acquired. In stereotype-threat research, making a negative group stereotype relevant has sometimes impaired performance by changing the meaning of a difficult task (Steele & Aronson, 1995; Spencer et al., 1999). Anxiety, monitoring, working-memory load, belonging, and motivation are plausible pathways, but effect estimates are heterogeneous across populations, tasks, manipulations, and settings; some operationally plausible estimates are small and evidence of publication bias matters (Flore & Wicherts, 2015; Shewach et al., 2019).
-
-Those meanings can alter attention and action alongside structural conditions, prior preparation, discrimination, and resources. [Chapter 29](29-culture-and-identity-the-same-action-is-not-the-same-act.qmd#chapter-29-start) develops identity and culture as hypotheses about meaning; here identity matters because it is one route through which an expectation can change the system.
-:::
-
-## Expectations between people
-
-Anticipating a difficult conversation can help us prepare. It can also change our manner in ways that make the other person harder to read. A relationship can deteriorate while each person believes they are simply responding to the other.
-
-Suppose you expect a colleague to be defensive. You enter the conversation cautiously, use guarded language, avoid warmth, and interpret questions as resistance. Your colleague senses distrust, becomes less open, and responds stiffly. You leave thinking, “I knew they would be defensive.”
-
-The colleague’s response could therefore reflect both an existing concern and a reaction to your guarded manner.
-
-Snyder, Tanke, and Berscheid (1977) demonstrated this kind of behavioral confirmation in a classic study. Men spoke by telephone with women whom they believed to be either physically attractive or unattractive, based on photographs that were not actually of the women. Men who believed they were speaking with an attractive woman behaved more warmly. The women, responding to that warmth, behaved in ways that independent observers judged as warmer and more sociable. The men’s expectations changed their own behavior, which elicited confirming behavior from the women.
-
-Darley and Fazio (1980) described expectancy-confirmation processes in social interaction. Expectations shape how perceivers act toward targets; targets respond to that treatment; perceivers interpret the response as evidence for their original expectation. The process can happen without conscious intention.
-
-The manager can apply this lesson without assuming the employee is already capable of everything. She could delegate one bounded assignment, specify the result, and agree on when help is needed. The response would then provide evidence about initiative under those conditions.
-
-In a negotiation, the corresponding step might be to ask a useful question while protecting sensitive information. A guarded reply can reflect the other party's aims, their response to your manner, or both. Changing one's own behavior creates an opportunity to learn.
-
-## Building truthful, supported expectations
-
-The manager’s bounded assignment shows how to turn a broad expectation into a specific, supported test. “This employee lacks initiative” is too broad; “Given a clear goal and authority over the method, will the employee propose a workable plan within a week?” identifies both the behavior and its conditions. Record the prediction before the assignment, provide the promised support, and compare the result with the original expectation.
-
-Separate an assessment from a decision about treatment. Evidence that someone currently lacks a skill does not determine whether they should receive training, and a temporary performance problem should not silently become a permanent identity. The relevant question is what opportunity would allow improvement and what evidence would show it.
-
-Support also has to be credible. Telling employees they are trusted while retaining every decision gives them conflicting instructions. @tbl-08-1 pairs each setting’s pathway with the support or safeguard it requires, from balanced risk information in medicine to credible authority and resources in management. A disappointing result may reflect the task, material constraints, discrimination, or luck; it cannot be used to conclude that the person failed to believe strongly enough.
-
-| Domain | Pathway | Ethical condition |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Medicine | Expectation changes attention, anxiety, and symptom experience. | Give balanced risk information and a credible coping plan. |
-| Education | Ability beliefs change effort, persistence, and interpretation of difficulty. | Pair high expectations with strategy, support, and opportunity. |
-| Management | Leader expectations change autonomy, feedback, and challenge. | Make resources and standards match the expectation. |
-| Relationships | Predicted rejection changes behavior and the response it elicits. | Test the prediction through perspective-getting, not mind-reading. |
-: Expectation pathways and ethical conditions {#tbl-08-1}
+A **reference class** supplies a starting rate from comparable cases; a causal account explains why this case might differ. [Chapter 14](14-base-rates-conditional-probability-and-bayesian-updating.qmd#reference-classes-are-judgment-calls) examines that comparison group, and [Chapter 15](15-samples-randomness-regression-and-calibration.qmd#calibration-makes-confidence-answerable) shows how to assess forecasts against outcomes.
+
+When one number hides important uncertainty, record a **base scenario** with the best-supported assumptions, an **upside scenario** with conditions for improvement, and a **downside scenario** with a plausible failure path. Each needs an observable sign and a decision that would follow. Forecasting success still does not determine whether a launch is worthwhile; that also depends on the value of its possible outcomes.
+
+Finally, record what changes when people receive or act on the forecast: effort, treatment by others, resources, or opportunities. Without that record, a forecast that partly produces its own confirmation can look like an independent prediction.
+:::
 
