 ## Why judgment systems fail: bias, noise, and misplaced intuition
+
+A striking experiment shows why adding information can make judgment worse. Dana, Dawes, and Peterson (2013) asked students to predict other students’ semester grades using background information, including prior grades. For one target, they also conducted an unstructured interview. In one condition, the interviewee secretly began answering closed questions using a rule based on letters in the questions rather than truthful answers. Interviewers still reported obtaining useful information; their confidence did not reliably distinguish these answers from truthful ones.
+
+Predictions made after interviews correlated less strongly with actual grades than predictions made from the background information alone: 0.31 versus 0.61 in the first study. The interviews supplied an intelligible impression that diluted useful evidence. This was a student-grade task, not a test of every employment interview. Its warning is nevertheless concrete: feeling that we understand a person can coexist with predicting that person less accurately.
 
 A hiring process can repeatedly favor the wrong cue, or it can produce different judgments depending on which qualified panel reviews the same evidence. Both can change who gets the job, but correcting the favored cue will not necessarily reduce the variation. Before redesigning the process, distinguish the direction of error from its inconsistency. Bias has a direction relative to a defensible target. A forecaster who is consistently too optimistic or a scale that always reads two kilograms high is biased when later outcomes or a calibrated standard supplies that target. In value-laden decisions, the benchmark must be specified and defended. **Noise** is unwanted variation among repeated, commensurable judgments that should be similar. If qualified teachers grade the same essay very differently, physicians use different thresholds for the same case, or managers rate the same candidate from 4 to 9 on a ten-point scale, the system is noisy (Kahneman et al., 2021).
 
